  "query_preview": "..\n\nThursday morning sees the beginning of a stronger atmospheric\nriver move into the area. Some rather impressive dynamics look to\nbe associated with this storm, with strong initial warm air\nadvection, and relatively cold air advecting eastward. These\ningredients coupled with sea surface temperatures still around \nthe mid 50s means that the environment leans more towards the\nconvective. Furthermore, a strong jet aloft with plenty of\ndivergence behind the warm front sets the stage for meso vertices\nforming along the front. These cause a number of issues: timing, \nlocation, and intensity of rainfall and highest winds can shift \ndrastically. Individual model runs tend to struggle with these \nenvironments, with run to run consistency of both the GFS and EC \ninconclusive on individual features. Ensemble guidance provides a \nclue on the location of highest wind speeds and rainfall however. \nAt this point, the southern half of the panhandle has the highest \nlikelihood of longer duration, heavier rainfall. The specifics \nregarding the hydrology portion of this storm are noted in the \nhydrology section. \n\nLooking towards the weekend, there is roughly a 60-40 split of\nactive weather continuing and a stronger low passing south\nrespectively. If active weather continues, then generally\nsoutherly winds will accompany mostly rain for the panhandle. For\nthe low passing south, this would invite stronger, downsloping\nnortherlies, which would dry out the panhandle and bring cooler\ntemperatures overnight.",
